keel (0.23.63) unstable; urgency=high

  * A primary whose server crashed and started again while it held the
    VIP acknowledged writes with no copy on the replica (keel#242). The
    replica's I/O thread connects again only at its next try, and with
    rpl_semi_sync_master_wait_no_slave OFF a commit with no replica
    connected is acknowledged at once. follow turned read_only off on
    such a holder at once, so a cut that came before the replica
    connected lost an acknowledged row (CI runs 452 and 482; the end to
    end test lost a row in 2 of 4 runs). Now follow, on a holder that is
    read only and replicates from nobody, waits until a replica is
    connected and Rpl_semi_sync_master_status is ON, for 30 s at most
    (the semi-synchronous timeout), and reads the VIP's proof again
    before read_only goes off. A promoted replica does not wait.

