keel (0.23.60) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A try-restart of keel-vip.service on the holder could remove the VIP
    from wg0 for a moment (keel#87; seen in vip-upgrade / trixie, on a
    link of 250 ms with 2% loss). The address keeps the lifetime of the
    old controller's last confirmed renewal, and the next controller's
    start and first renewal took longer than what was left. keel vip
    tend --stopped now renews the lease once, as the controller does (a
    keep-alive, then a linearizable read), before it lets the unit
    restart, and gives the address the lifetime that renewal leaves.
    A single deadline (CALL_TIMEOUT) caps the two calls, so the restart
    takes about 1.5 s on a 250 ms link, not the 3.8 s two full timeouts
    took. With no answer from etcd the address stays as it was; a lease
    gone drops it. The vip-upgrade test reports the valid_lft right
    after each restart, so a run that fails names its cause.

 -- Marcos Méndez <popsolutions.co@gmail.com>  Wed, 29 Oct 2026 03:10:00 +0000

keel (0.23.59) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A write in flight at a planned promote waited 15 s for its answer,
    and the new primary took 10 s to take writes (keel#138). The 10 s
    were the announcement to a member outside the pair that was down,
    which keel#137 takes out of the handover. The write: the old primary
    dropped the VIP while the commit waited for the replica's
    acknowledgement, so the answer had no address to come from. Now the
    old primary, when it declares a database, turns it read only before
    it releases the VIP: SET GLOBAL read_only = ON returns once the
    commits in flight ended, which the replica, still connected,
    acknowledges; then the address goes. A failure is logged and the
    release goes on.
  * With etcd, a planned promote waited for the controller's next lease
    renewal before the VIP was on wg0 (a 5.6 s write gap at 250 ms).
    The majority granted the lease and wrote the claim with it, as a
    renewal confirms it, so the promote now adds the address itself,
    for what is left of the release time after the lease was asked for;
    the controller extends it once it renewed, and the promote checks
    that renewal before it returns. A primary that released the VIP
    dropped the address and revoked its own lease first, so the promote
    claims at once instead of waiting for that lease to go.

 -- Marcos Méndez <popsolutions.co@gmail.com>  Tue, 28 Oct 2026 20:45:00 +0000

keel (0.23.58) unstable; urgency=medium

  * In cloud simple, one member outside the pair that is down made every
    keel vip promote wait two 10 s timeouts, for its epoch and for the
    announcement: a planned promote took about 21 s on a real pair
    (keel#137). Now the other member of the pair alone gates the
    handover: its epoch, its release and its taking the claim, each
    waited for in full, as nothing fences a live old primary without
    etcd. The members outside the pair are told after, in the
    background, and what each said is logged. With --old-primary-gone
    every peer is asked and told, and the claim is carried on a
    majority of those that answer, as before. With etcd the peers are
    told in the background too. A replica that holds the primary's
    claim asks the release at the next epoch at once, and asks the
    epoch only when the primary refuses that as stale: two round trips
    with the other member, not three.

