keel (0.23.52) unstable; urgency=medium

  * File replication with syncthing, one operation with the database
    role's (0050 and its amendment of 2026-10-20): the appliance
    manifest's state.replicate is one syncthing folder per path in
    cloud simple and cloud advanced, send-only on the proven primary,
    receive-only on the replica with simple versioning, paused while a
    node is in doubt or not proven. state.exclude is the folder's
    .stignore. The device ID is made on the node once and asked of the
    pair's other member at each flip in a new signed `device` vipmsg
    kind, answered only to the pair member, with and without etcd.
    Every flip first writes the folders' state (paused, primary or
    replica) to /run/keel/filesync/state; apply renders from that file
    only, and a missing file means paused, so apply never sends again
    what a flip paused. The state does not survive a reboot, and the
    unit's drop-in has an ExecStartPre that pauses every folder in
    config.xml when there is no state, so a node that was primary at
    shutdown sends nothing before a follow proves it. A follow that proves no role pauses the
    folders at every exit. A running syncthing gets the type and the
    pause through its REST API and keeps running; the configuration
    file stays for a cold start. Where the API gives no answer,
    syncthing restarts and keel reads the folders back. A flip or a
    role hook that fails is a follow problem, and keel database watch
    follows again until it is done. The API key goes in a request
    header, never in a command line. Manifest paths are checked before
    they reach the configuration, the unit's drop-in or a .stignore.
    The GUI and the REST API listen on the loopback with an API key
    root only; the unit's drop-in grants write access to the home and
    the replicated paths only, each with the "-" prefix. After the flip
    each executable of /usr/lib/keel/roles.d/ runs with the role, with
    a minimal environment. keel diff shows the direction, the state and
    the peer of each folder and the counts receiveOnlyChangedFiles and
    needFiles; one alert goes out for each change of the set of counts
    (needFiles after three runs in a row). A node that replicates no
    path sees no change: no identity, no ask, no diff rows.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0000

