keel (0.23.49) unstable; urgency=medium

  * postgresql@.service stops with a fast shutdown and in at most 120 s
    (keel#219): a drop-in, keel-stop.conf, sets KillSignal=SIGINT and
    TimeoutStopSec=120s. A stop that arrives while the start still runs does
    not run ExecStop, and systemd sent SIGTERM to the postmaster: a smart
    shutdown, which waits for every client. An idle client (the poll of
    Odoo's serve.py, a psql of keel) held it for TimeoutStopSec, 1 h in
    Debian's unit; one bootstrap of a pair waited 15 minutes. Measured on a
    real standby with one idle client: SIGTERM, the postmaster alive after
    12 s; SIGINT, gone in under 5 s. SIGINT is a fast shutdown, safe for
    committed data, and the final SIGKILL after 120 s is crash safe because
    PostgreSQL recovers from WAL at the next start.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 07:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.48) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The stall that ends the drain of a PostgreSQL promotion is 2 s, not 5 s
    (keel#215, keel 0.23.46). The cost of a cut that ends the stream inside
    a record was 5 s: the first database write came 6.79 s after the VIP.
    The condition did not change: the drain ends by stall only when neither
    the replayed nor the received position moved for the whole stall, so a
    replay that still moves never ends it. Replay is local, and only the
    received position can stall because of the network; a received position
    that stands still while replay moves keeps the wait. Tests with random
    positions, run with 5 s and with 2 s, check that. If replay stands still
    for 2 s with whole records pending (a query that blocks it on a
    standby), pg_promote() still replays them before it ends recovery.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.47) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Before the rejoin of an old PostgreSQL primary, keel estimates what
    pg_rewind would copy (the WAL files of the holder from the segment of
    the fork, and the WAL that the old primary wrote after it) and what
    a base backup would copy, and times the link. A rewind that takes 120 s
    or less is taken. Otherwise keel picks the smaller one (handbook
    decision 0054, second amendment of 2026-10-18). On a link of 250 ms and
    1.5 % loss the rewind of a small cluster took 21 minutes and the
    reseed 2.4 minutes.
  * The reseed that keel picks needs no --destroy-local-database when the
    holder holds the newest claim of the VIP and this node is fenced. With
    no room to keep a copy of the old data, the flag is required.
  * A reseed keeps the old data directory as <datadir>.keel-kept-<stamp>,
    says the path and sends an alert. keel keeps the newest copy and
    deletes the others after 7 days or when the free space is low.
  * keel sends an alert when pg_rewind fails after it began to copy
    (keel#214).

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000

keel (0.23.46) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The drain of a PostgreSQL promotion ends when neither the replayed
    nor the received position has moved for 5 s (keel#215). A cut can end
    the stream inside a record: the walreceiver flushes the bytes that
    arrived, replay stands at the end of the last whole record, and the two
    positions never meet. On a real pair the standby that took the VIP
    waited 417 s (received 0/7EC8000, replayed 0/7EC7A58, one TCP segment
    apart). pg_promote() replays every whole record that is in pg_wal
    before it ends recovery, so only the unfinished record is left behind,
    and no standby can apply that. A standby whose positions still move
    keeps its wait, and a standby that receives nothing is still refused.
    Follow says how many bytes it left behind.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:45:00 +0000

keel (0.23.45) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An old PostgreSQL primary rejoins with pg_rewind against the new
    primary when it can, and no longer needs a full reseed (handbook
    decision 0054, amendment of 2026-10-18). Follow dumps the fenced
    node, stops it, runs pg_rewind, and starts it as a standby on the
    new timeline. If pg_rewind refuses before the copy begins, nothing
    changes and the confirmed reseed stays the fallback. A rewind that
    fails halfway marks the copy unusable until the reseed. There is no
    dry run: it reads as much as the run does.
  * The pair drop-in adds wal_keep_size = 256MB. An apply of the older
    drop-in writes it and reloads the server; it never restarts it.
  * The holder grants the replication role the four functions that
    pg_rewind reads files through, in the postgres database only.

