    Now it shows the role of the server, the timeline, the synchronous
    commit and synchronous_standby_names, each standby that streams from
    the node (address, state, sync state, replay lag), each replication
    slot (active or not, restart_lsn, wal_status), what a standby
    receives and its replay lag, the certificate and a divergence. One
    psql question as the postgres account, read only.
  * On the VIP holder, keel database status and keel spec apply named
    the other member as the primary's address (keel#155, item 2). The
    holder is now its own primary. A node that released, or was fenced,
    while its own claim is the newest it knows still names the other
    member.
  * A test finds a debian/changelog entry with no trailer line (keel#155,
    item 3). dpkg-parsechangelog only warns about one, and the package
    builds. The entries of 0.23.18 and 0.23.17 had none until 0.23.21.

