keel (0.23.28) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel verify checks the signature of a layer .hash file against the
    keyrings of the main and the images roles, which
    keel-archive-keyring 0.3.0 installs (decision 0055). A signature by
    either key is good. A file that main and images both signed
    verifies with only one of the 2 keyrings, because keel reads the
    status lines of gpgv one signature at a time and not its exit code.
    A good signature gives "ok: hash file signed by <fingerprint>", and
    the digests come from the signed text. A BADSIG is a mismatch.
  * keel verify --release-manifest FILE verifies a release MANIFEST
    against FILE.asc with the same keyrings, then the size and sha256 of
    each listed file that is present.
  * keel verify --keyring FILE, which can repeat, and
    $KEEL_IMAGE_KEYRINGS replace the 2 default keyrings.

