keel (0.23.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel's PostgreSQL client asked psql for its aligned output. psql
    pads the columns with spaces and a bar, and keel's readers split a
    row on the tab, so no answer split into its columns: every keel
    database promote read "not in recovery", the seed's probe could not
    see a standby, and the holder's timeline was never read (found
    when the pair's network-namespace test's cases (b) to (e) were
    built; case (a) never asked these questions). The client
    now asks for unaligned output with a tab between the columns, the
    way inspect asks. It also stops at the first failed statement on
    its standard input: psql went on and exited 0, so a refused
    pg_promote() or role statement read as done. The replication
    role's statement does nothing on a standby now, where the role
    arrives with the copy and ALTER ROLE is refused.
  * A promoted standby had no replication slot: pg_basebackup copies
    no slot, and nothing made the pair's slot on the new primary. The
    reseed of the old primary names that slot, so it never streamed.
    keel database follow now makes the slot on the holder (the
    statement does nothing when the slot is there, and commits without
    the standby).
  * An old PostgreSQL primary that came back holding data stayed a
    writable primary: keel recorded its divergence and said "read
    only", and the next follow started the server that the unproven
    claim had stopped. keel database follow now fences it first: the
    server becomes a standby that follows nobody (standby.signal, no
    follow drop-in, a restart), so it refuses every write until the
    confirmed reseed. Its timeline is read in recovery too, as a
    number like the holder's, and the dump is taken once per
    divergence: the record names it, and a fenced node takes no write
    since. keel diff and keel database status show the two timelines
    of a PostgreSQL divergence, where they said "unknown".
  * keel database watch alerts while a PostgreSQL primary's commits
    wait for the standby, as 0054 asks: when no standby streams from
    it, or when a commit waited more than 10 s. It alerts once, and
    once more when the standby acknowledges again. Before, the watch
    said nothing for PostgreSQL, and a cut standby stopped every write
    without a word.
  * The seed of a PostgreSQL standby copies less: the primary
    compresses the base backup (zstd), and pg_basebackup unpacks it.
    A fresh cluster is about 40 MB raw and under 3 MB compressed, and
    the design case's link carries about 50 kB/s on one connection. In
    the pair's network-namespace test, B's first seed took about 12
    minutes.
  * The seed's copy took the standby's application name, keel. The
    WAL stream of pg_basebackup reports what it flushed, so the primary
    took the half-made copy for the pair's synchronous standby: during
    the reseed of an old primary, the new primary acknowledged commits
    that only the copy held (found by the pair's network-namespace
    test, case (d)). keel's own replication connections are named
    keel-seed now; only the standby is keel.
  * keel database promote --old-primary-gone on a PostgreSQL standby
    lets the new primary commit alone: the one exception to 0054's
    no-fallback rule. The operator's flag says the old primary is
    gone, so a commit has no second node to wait for, and without the
    exception every write waited until the old primary was reseeded
    (216 s in the pair's network-namespace test, case (c)). keel
    empties synchronous_standby_names in the pair drop-in and reloads
    the server (no restart); the watch alerts at each run while the
    primary commits alone; and once a standby streams again, keel
    writes FIRST 1 (keel) back. Without the flag nothing changes: a
    planned promote and a cut replica keep the synchronous wait.
  * The PostgreSQL pair's end to end test runs cases (b) to (e) of
    docs/replication-postgresql.md under the design case (0050), in
    the CI job "postgres / trixie": the replica cut off (a commit
    waits, the watch alerts, the commit finishes after the heal), the
    primary cut off and killed while the application writes (the
    operator's promote, no acknowledged row lost, the downtime), the
    old primary back (it acknowledges no write, it is fenced, dumped,
    recorded and alerted once) and its confirmed reseed.

