keel (0.23.17) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel's PostgreSQL client carried MariaDB's --batch, which psql has
    not, so every psql keel ran refused to start (found by the pair's
    network-namespace test of this release): the flag is out, and
    --tuples-only was already there.
  * keel database watch renewed the pair's database certificate without
    the pair's signed record, so the root's holder refused every renewal
    (a certificate for the VIP needs the pair's signed record; found by
    the pair's network-namespace test of this release); the watch also
    read the leaf's expiry and refreshed the CRL in the MariaDB layout
    whatever the node declared, and FLUSH SSL is a MariaDB statement:
    the watch now sends the record, reads the engine's layout, and
    flushes only a MariaDB server (a running PostgreSQL takes the new
    CRL at its next restart). The TLS action's line no longer names
    /etc/mysql/keel-tls on a PostgreSQL node.
  * the pair's replication line asked scram-sha-256 for the
    certificate's map: pg refuses map= with a password method and
    the server dies loading the file (found by the pair's
    network-namespace test of this release). The pair's line is now
    the cert method — the certificate's CN mapped through
    pg_ident pins the peer, the password stays on the standalone
    lines; and clientcert=verify-full came out of that line: it
    asks the client host's name of a DNS the mesh has not, and
    certificate authentication failed for the pair's role (found
    by the pair's network-namespace test of this release). The
    map is the pinning, the twin of MariaDB's REQUIRE SUBJECT.
  * the pair's hba gave the replication role no database to ask:
    the reach and seed probes connect as the replication role to
    the server's own postgres database (does it answer, does the
    role authenticate, is it a standby), the file carried the role
    only for the replication pseudo-database, the seed was refused
    and the pair never formed (found by the pair's
    network-namespace test of this release). Each origin now
    carries one postgres-database line for the role, scram-sha-256
    over TLS; user data stays unreachable.
  * the pair's slot and role statements committed WAL under the
    pair's synchronous commit policy: PG 17 writes the slot's
    creation to WAL, so the statement's commit waited for the
    synchronous standby that did not exist yet — the first apply of a
    pair never finished (found by the pair's network-namespace test of
    this release). The statements now set synchronous_commit = local
    first, the way a bootstrap that lets the standby connect must.
  * the pair's pg_hba.conf carried the other member's address bare:
    pg_hba.conf reads a bare token as a hostname, the method lands in
    the mask's place, and the server dies loading the file (found by
    the pair's network-namespace test of this release). An origin that
    is an IP address now takes the host mask (/128 or /32).
  * the pair's PostgreSQL drop-in named the TLS files unquoted:
    postgresql.conf rejects a value that starts with /, the server
    dies at boot reading its configuration (found by the pair's
    network-namespace test of this release), so the ssl file lines
    quote the paths, the way hba_file and ident_file already do.
  * the pair's hba and ident files were drop-ins in the cluster's
    conf.d: Debian's postgresql.conf includes that directory, every
    *.conf there is read as GUC, neither file is GUC, and the server
    dies at boot (found by the pair's network-namespace test of this
    release). They now live in the cluster's configuration directory,
    the way Debian keeps pg_hba.conf; only GUC drop-ins stay in
    conf.d.
  * A database pair of PostgreSQL (handbook decision 0054): the pair
    converge, the follow of the VIP, the network-namespace gate.
    This change is the TLS layer: the database leaf of the mesh root
    is parameterized by engine, not forked — the directory (etc/
    postgresql/keel-tls), the file group postgres, the certificate
    subject <member> postgresql and the libpq option names differ;
    the PKI flow is the same, and the request carries the engine word,
    which the root's holder validates. keel.system.dbpostgres gains
    the pair's pure functions: the pair drop-in (synchronous
    standby of application_name keel, no timeout; wal_log_hints for
    the pg_rewind revisit), the idempotent replication slot keel, the
    hba clientcert=verify-full lines with the keel map of a written
    pg_ident.conf, and the slot in the follow drop-in.
  * The PostgreSQL pair's follow of the VIP (0054): a primary whose
    standby claim is not proven is stopped (a primary has no read-only
    boot, so an unproven primary may not keep serving); a proven standby
    holder is drained (the replay caught up to the received WAL) and
    promoted with pg_promote(); a replica holding no user database is
    seeded with pg_basebackup over TLS without asking; one holding data
    is dumped first (pg_dumpall, compressed under /var/backups/keel/
    postgresql/), reseeds on --destroy-local-database, and until then
    its divergence (the timelines it forked from the holder's) is
    recorded in /var/lib/keel/database/diverged and it is not touched.
    The rejoin after a promotion is the confirmed reseed and nothing
    else: no pg_rewind and no resume, 0054's rule, so wal_log_hints
    does not come back.
  * The PostgreSQL pair's end to end test under the design case (0050),
    the CI job "postgres / trixie": tests/postgres_netns.py runs the
    pair's case (a) of docs/replication-postgresql.md on tests/
    vip_netns.py's three nodes and trixie's PostgreSQL as a server per
    node in its network namespace — the seed over TLS with the
    walreceiver's sslmode=verify-full and the pair's slot, the
    synchronous commit (a commit pays the standby's acknowledgement,
    one round trip), a write on the primary through its loopback read
    on the replica, the replica refusing writes while it is in
    recovery, the other member's database leaf taken and its etcd leaf
    refused, keel diff clean on both. The failover, the rejoin and the
    diverged old primary are the follow-up pull request's.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.16) unstable; urgency=medium

  * After the upgrade to 0.23.13, wg0.conf had MTU = 1280 and the live
    wg0 kept 1420 until a reboot (keel#139): only wg-quick up reads the
    line, apply planned nothing and keel diff did not see it. The live
    change of the overlay now sets the file's MTU with ip link set,
    with no restart. A kept file whose interface has another MTU gets
    the same action in keel spec apply, said as drift. keel network mtu
    does it for each WireGuard interface that is up, and the package's
    postinst runs it, so an upgrade needs no reboot. keel diff reports
    network.overlay.wireguard.mtu: the live MTU against the file's.
  * A handshake older than 180 s is drift in keel diff and keel mesh
    status, not same (keel#140): WireGuard does a new handshake every 2
    minutes while packets flow. The drift says the member does not
    answer and is unreachable, and no longer tells the operator to run
    keel mesh sync on it.
  * The PostgreSQL converge begins (handbook decision 0054, the second
    engine after MariaDB under 0013's one-at-a-time rule):
    keel.system.dbpostgres is the pure twin of keel.system.dbmariadb:
    the versioned cluster paths resolved from the root, the drop-in
    text (hba_file moves the whole authorization surface into keel's
    file, a primary carries wal_level and the sender and slot limits),
    the pg_hba text per declared origin, the primary_conninfo follow
    drop-in with its two-layer password escaping, the pg_promote()
    statement, and the literal escaping. The dispatch lands on it:
    database.server of engine postgresql converges standalone, primary
    and replica the way MariaDB does, with the engine's own pieces: the
    drop-in and the hba file written by one step (a changed hba alone is
    reloaded, a changed drop-in restarts), the idempotent replication
    role statement when the authorized origins drift, the base-backup
    seed (keel.system.dbpgseed: pg_basebackup into a fresh directory,
    standby.signal and the follow drop-in beside it, the old data
    directory swapped back on any failure before the server starts) and
    the drain-then-pg_promote() promotion (keel.system.dbpgpromote: the
    replayed WAL position must reach the received one, compared as
    numbers, and a standby that receives nothing is refused). A standby
    refuses writes while it recovers, so there is no read_only lock step
    and no unlock on promotion.

