keel (0.23.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The overlay's file sets MTU = 1280 on each node (keel#119). Before,
    wg-quick took the MTU of the route to an endpoint less 80. A node
    on an uplink of 1500 bytes got 1420, and a node behind PPPoE got
    1412. WireGuard pads each packet up to the MTU of its interface, so
    the node of 1420 sent full TCP segments as 1500 bytes on the wire.
    The path to the PPPoE site is 1492 bytes and dropped them. When the
    ICMPv6 "packet too big" did not come in time, the members' channel
    stopped at its 30 s timeout: "did not answer: timed out", with fresh
    handshakes. 1280 is the minimum MTU of IPv6, which is 1360 bytes on
    the wire.
  * A change of the MTU line is a change of [Interface]: wg-quick down,
    then up, under the window. The first apply of this version on a node
    of an earlier version does this one time. A change of the peers
    alone stays live with wg set.
  * keel inspect reads MTU = 1280 as keel's own line. It names another
    MTU value in the report.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * After keel vip promote --old-primary-gone the new primary refused
    writes for 11.7 s while it carried the VIP (keel#118). keel database
    follow promoted the database 7.5 s after the VIP landed: STOP SLAVE
    IO_THREAD waits until the I/O thread ends, which with a dead primary
    takes up to 10 s (a try to connect) or 5 s (the semi-synchronous
    kill of the dump thread). The thread receives nothing once the stop
    marks it killed. The promotion now runs the stop in a client of its
    own; once the stop is in the server's killing phase, the SQL thread
    drains, the received position and the killing phase are checked
    again, and read_only goes off; the I/O thread is
    waited for and the primary forgotten after. When anything
    arrived after the drain, the old order applies. A planned promote
    takes the same path. Spec apply's replication actions take follow's
    lock. Follow looks for the VIP every 0.2 s, not 0.5 s.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * With etcd, two nodes could carry the VIP for less than a second
    (keel#126). etcd gives a lease's time to live in whole seconds,
    rounded down, so a lease with less than 1 s left shows 0, and a
    renewal in that second keeps it. The other node of the pair took 0
    as a lease that is gone and claimed. A holder that came back from a
    cut off in the last second of its lease renewed it and carried the
    VIP, beside the new holder, until it took the newer claim from the
    counter. Now the other node, and keel vip promote, claim only when
    etcd shows -1: the lease expired at etcd's leader, and no renewal
    can keep it. The old holder's address is gone 10 s or more before
    a claim is possible; docs/vip.md gives the margin on the design
    case. The VIP's netns tests also watch the nodes in a tight loop
    and fail on any instant two nodes carry the VIP.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000

keel (0.23.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel vip promote --old-primary-gone took 30.5 s on a real pair with
    the old primary dead: it waited three times for the members'
    channel's 10 s timeout, for the epoch, the release and the
    announcement (keel#108). With --old-primary-gone and etcd formed,
    each ask of the old primary now waits at most 1.5 s, and the other
    peers are not held up; the promote still waits for the old
    primary's lease to go and never revokes it (decision 0049). The ask
    goes on after the bound and the promote waits for it before it
    returns, so a slow live old primary still gets the release. Without
    etcd nothing fences a live old primary, so its asks keep the full
    timeout; the help and docs/vip.md say that such an old primary stays
    writable until it learns the newer claim.
