keel (0.23.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A node that joins a mesh built by hand gets all the members as peers
    (keel#99). The members of such a mesh are trust roots of each other
    and have no admission evidence, so the joiner took only the inviter.
    The other members took the joiner from the announcement and waited
    for a handshake that did not come. Now a member names its operator
    roots (made by --adopt, or the inviter of its own invite) in the
    join's answer and in its roster. A node takes them from an operator
    root of its own as named roots, says "member X is a named trust
    root, named by R", and binds their signing keys from their own
    rosters. The same applies to a node that a member other than the
    first node invites. A node that joined before this version gets the
    missing members at its next keel mesh sync, when its inviter has
    this version.
  * A named root signs admission evidence, but names nobody: root
    status never goes beyond one hop. It removes no other node, here or
    mesh-wide, has no certificate revoked at the root CA's holder, signs
    no VIP pair record for another, is no source of the root CA's
    anchor and gives no node its first etcd certificate. When its namer
    has a tombstone, it is a plain member again. --adopt never makes a
    key with a tombstone a root.
  * keel mesh remove of an operator root of this node asks the rosters
    of the other peers first. A root is not always a root both ways, so
    it refuses, and changes nothing, when a peer holds that root as a
    root and does not hold this node as one, or when a peer does not
    answer.
  * The join tells how many members it took. It names on standard error
    each member that it did not take, and it does not say "this node has
    them all" when that is not true.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0000

