keel (0.23.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel mesh invite draws a random free address of the inviter's
    region, the /112 of the overlay its own address is in (decision
    0051, keel#97), instead of one of the /64. With etcd, the invite
    also reserves the address there by a compare-and-swap, on a lease
    that ends a day after the invite, and the join is admitted only
    while that reservation names it, so two members of one region that
    invite at once never give out one address (keel#102). Addresses already given are untouched: a node
    placed in the /64 before regions allocates in its own /112.
  * VIPs live in the overlay's VIP range, <prefix>::ffff:n, outside
    every region: a pair's members are normally in two regions and
    either carries the VIP. appliance.vip, the pair record and every
    claim are checked against the range; no invite allocates from it,
    and no node address or peer allowed_ips of a spec may lie in it. A
    node whose address lies in it cannot invite.
  * Upgrade: a VIP paired before 0.23.3, outside the range, stays valid
    when a signed pair record names it; it validates, applies and is
    claimed as before, and keel diff prints one information line. A new
    pair outside the range is refused.
  * With etcd, keel vip pair reserves the VIP by a compare-and-swap
    before the other member signs, and the other member signs only a
    pair whose VIP is reserved for it, so two pairs never take one VIP.
    The reservation is signed by the member that reserves, and every
    reader checks it: an unsigned or forged one is an alert, never
    overwritten. It lives on a lease of 24 h until both members signed
    the record. keel vip unpair VIP releases it. The member role may
    write /keel/<mesh>/vips/.
  * docs/vip.md: keel vip pair ADDRESS takes the other node's overlay
    address, not the VIP; the replication and spec examples agree.

