keel (0.23.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A MariaDB pair on the mesh (decisions 0013, 0020, 0031, 0049; the
    maintainer's answers of 2026-10-03 and 2026-10-11; docs/replication.md).
    On a node that declares appliance.vip the primary is the VIP's holder:
    database.server.role is the installation's, decides who seeds from
    whom before the first claim, and is runtime state from then on;
    inspect reports the VIP's role, diff shows the difference as
    information, apply never converges it. With the pair record,
    replication.primary, allowed_from and secret are derived and optional.
  * Semi-synchronous replication: AFTER_SYNC, a 10 s timeout, one replica,
    both sides enabled on both nodes; the fallback to asynchronous is drift
    in keel diff (database.server.semi_sync) and alerted once, with its
    recovery, by keel database watch (keel-database-watch.timer, 30 s).
  * The replica serves nothing: read_only, and READ_ONLY ADMIN revoked
    from root too, so Webmin's module and root at a shell get 1290; keel's
    own statements go through 'mysql'@'localhost' (runuser -u mysql).
  * TLS on replication beside WireGuard: a database leaf per node from the
    mesh root CA (CN no etcd user, SANs the node's /128 and the VIP), the
    root made by keel mesh create in every cloud mode, REQUIRE X509 on the
    replication account, MASTER_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT; renewed by watch.
  * The replication credential is made on the primary and asked for by
    the other member over the members' channel (POST /v1/vip, secret),
    the first shared secret of 0041; never typed.
  * Both nodes bind the VIP through net.ipv6.ip_nonlocal_bind, so the
    primary answers on it the moment it carries the address and no role
    change restarts MariaDB; one drop-in for both nodes.
  * keel database follow: the database follows the VIP, run by
    keel-database-follow.path when the VIP's state changes and at boot:
    the holder drained and promoted, the other node read only, locked and
    following the holder, an old primary rejoined by GTID when it holds
    nothing the holder lacks (a dump kept first), else left read only,
    reported (database.server.diverged, keel database status) and alerted;
    --destroy-local-database reseeds it. keel database promote moves the
    VIP, then follows. A write in flight when a primary is cut off commits
    after the 10 s and is errant: such a primary comes back diverged.
  * keel database status, one screen for the operator; the role file
    /var/lib/keel/role for tracker#57.
  * tests/test_mariadb_netns.py, the CI job mariadb / trixie: three nodes
    on 250 ms ±25 ms with 2% loss, a MariaDB server per node.

