keel (0.20.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The service VIP of a replicated appliance pair on the WireGuard mesh
    (handbook decision 0049, its third round): appliance.vip in the spec,
    a /128 of the overlay prefix the primary carries on wg0 and every
    other node routes to it through the primary's allowed_ips. The
    primary is the VIP's holder; there is no role field. A move is one
    wg set per node and the address on the holder, outside 0018's window.
  * keel vip promote: the old primary releases the VIP before this node
    takes it at the next epoch, and every peer is told on the members'
    channel (POST /v1/vip, signed claims checked against the trust
    store). It refuses while the old primary does not answer, unless
    --old-primary-gone. keel database promote moves the VIP first.
  * With etcd, keel vip tend (keel-vip.service of keel-overlay-vip): the
    holder renews a lease of 20 s every 2 s and drops the VIP after 10 s
    without a renewal; a claim is a compare-and-swap on the counter key,
    so a stale claim is never written; the other node of the pair claims
    once the lease expired, and a node that lost the VIP never claims it
    again by itself. keel vip check, at boot and every minute, learns
    newer claims from the peers and drops a VIP not held.
  * Only the pair holds its VIP: keel vip pair records the pair, signed
    by both members (or a trust root); every claim carries the record and
    every claim and release is checked against it; a VIP that is a
    member's address or outside its region's /112, and an epoch more than
    64 above the one a node knows, are refused. A promote before etcd
    carries the VIP only when a majority of the peers that answered took
    its claim, or, with --old-primary-gone, when none answered. keel mesh invite never hands out a VIP.
  * keel-vip.service is split as keel#75 splits an invite: keel vip tend,
    the root helper, alone changes wg0 after checking the pair record, and
    starts keel vip control, the controller facing etcd and the overlay,
    as a transient unit with a dynamic user and no capability, its socket
    an abstract socket of their namespace, under a fresh random name the
    helper binds first, each end checked by SO_PEERCRED, the client key handed as a memfd; the helper holds
    CAP_NET_ADMIN alone and sets no-new-privileges on itself. The controller carries the VIP only while the
    last renewal the majority confirmed is under 10 s old, timed on
    CLOCK_BOOTTIME in memory: a restarted or rebooted holder carries
    nothing until it renewed its lease again, and any error of etcd is no
    renewal. With etcd a claim carries, signed into it, the ID of its
    lease: the holder decides only by its own lease, and the other node
    claims only once the lease of the newest claim it verified is gone,
    by one transaction on the counter, never because the holder key was
    deleted or rewritten; a lease that ended early (revoked) is followed
    by a grace of 14 s, so the holder has dropped the VIP first, and a
    holder whose lease was revoked while it renewed it with the majority
    claims again at the next epoch, so revokes in a row never leave the
    pair fenced. etcd's
    access control is not enabled: this is defence in depth until it
    can be.
  * keel vip status and keel mesh status show the role, the epoch and
    holder, the lease, whether wg0 carries the VIP and where it is
    routed; keel inspect reports appliance.vip and the same as machine
    state, and reads the peers back without the VIP, so a move is never
    drift. apply renders wg0.conf with the VIP routed to its holder.
  * Tests: the etcd tests date their fixed clock by the real one, which
    the certificates openssl issues carry.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel (0.19.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * etcd, the mesh's registry (handbook decisions 0025 and 0048, its
    third round), on the cloud advanced members, over the WireGuard
    mesh. etcd uses its own TLS, peer and client, on the overlay
    addresses and ::1 only: the first node makes the mesh's root CA,
    every member holds an intermediate CA its inviter signs, carried in
    the join's authenticated answer, and issues its own member and
    client certificates. The member list and the certificates are state
    under /var/lib/keel/etcd; the spec says only overlays.etcd: enabled,
    which spec validate refuses outside cloud advanced.
  * etcd forms at the third cloud advanced member's join: the inviter
    writes the cluster of three, sends it to the third member over the
    members' channel, signed with its Ed25519 key, and each enables etcd
    in its own spec. From the fourth, the inviter adds the new node as a
    learner before it answers, and promotes it once in sync.
  * keel mesh etcd form forms etcd on a mesh that never saw a third join
    (one built with keel 0.18 and adopted, with no CA yet): it asks
    every member first, refuses two roots or a cluster it is not in,
    makes the root when none exists, enrolls every member before it
    starts anything, and with --dry-run only says what it would do. On a
    formed cluster it brings in the members it lacks. The fallback's
    join is brought in the same way.
  * keel mesh etcd tend, from keel-mesh-etcd.timer every 5 minutes,
    promotes learners in sync, removes those that never started within
    an hour, and renews the leaves with a third of their life left.
  * keel mesh remove also removes the etcd member, only when this node
    admitted the node or it is a trust root (the admission evidence
    amendment); otherwise the removal is local only.
  * keel mesh status shows etcd's members, leader and health.
  * apply renders /etc/default/etcd, the certificates for the etcd user
    and a sandboxing drop-in from the state, and starts etcd with
    --no-block; before the cluster exists etcd waits, without failing
    the run, and keel diff does not count it as drift. Heartbeat 300 ms,
    election timeout 5000 ms, for 250 ms ±25 ms with 2% loss
    (decision 0050), measured in tests/test_etcd_netns.py.
  * Only the root CA's holder forms the cluster, once: it signs a
    formation record (token, members, root fingerprint) with the root
    and reserves it under its lock, and a member takes a cluster only
    with that record. Another inviter of the third member admits it and
    names the holder for keel mesh etcd form, which runs only there.
  * Short lives and a CRL: leaves 30 days, renewed by their member;
    intermediates a year, renewed by the root's holder, which signs
    them with the root (an inviter relays the request), so chains stay
    one deep. Every intermediate is name constrained to the mesh's /64
    and ::1. The holder's CRL, signed by the root, goes to etcd's
    --peer-crl-file and --client-crl-file and travels with the rosters;
    keel mesh remove has the holder revoke the removed node's
    intermediates.
  * Every intermediate is signed by the root, the request relayed by
    the inviter (0048's third round, point 1, narrowed); one
    intermediate deep, path length 0, name constrained to its member's
    own /128 and ::1, and recorded with its address and key. When the
    holder cannot be reached the join completes without etcd, and
    keel-mesh-etcd.timer asks again. A revocation names no serial: the
    holder revokes what it recorded for that address and key, only for
    the node's admitter, a trust root or the node itself. Records carry
    etcd's cluster ID, an epoch, a nonce and an expiry, name exactly the
    cluster's members, and are never taken twice or older.
  * A renewal that fails is alerted through the monitor's channels and
    shown by keel mesh status and keel diff (etcd.certificates), as is a
    certificate within seven days of its expiry.
  * etcd's /health on a plain metrics listener on [::1]:2381, for
    Monit; apply refuses a member etcd-server's own start left, and the
    join or form that starts etcd removes it first only when
    keel-overlay-etcd marked it as its own.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel (0.18.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel mesh makes a full mesh before etcd (handbook decision 0048,
    "Until etcd exists"). A test on three Keel Web nodes across two
    sites found a star: a node that joined through one member was never
    learned by the others. The join's answer already named the members
    the inviter knows; the new node now takes them as peers in its
    first change. The inviter, once a join is confirmed, announces the
    new node to each of its other peers over the overlay, and each adds
    it under the window of decision 0018, confirmed by a WireGuard
    handshake from the new node. A member offline then, and a node that
    joined through the fallback, pull the members with the new
    keel mesh sync, which keel-mesh-sync.timer runs at boot and every
    15 minutes. A member that never answered is tried again after an
    hour, and announcements waiting are applied in one change, under
    one window.
  * Only an admitted join adds a peer. Each node has an Ed25519 signing
    key made on the machine; an inviter signs evidence of each
    admission (the mesh, the invite, the node's keys, address and
    endpoint, the time), sent in the join's authenticated answer and
    with the node's entry in every roster. A member takes a roster's
    entry only on evidence of an invite signed by a key it already
    trusts: its own, its inviter's, a member admitted the same way, or
    a trust root that keel mesh create --adopt or keel mesh sync
    --adopt made of the spec's peers, the operator's act for a mesh
    built by hand, whose signing key is bound only from a roster the
    node fetched from the root itself. docs/mesh.md says what this
    changes in 0048's threat model; rotating a signing key is follow-up
    work.
  * keel mesh remove removes a node from the mesh before etcd: a
    tombstone signed with the node's key, the peer out of its spec
    under the window, confirmed by a handshake from a peer it keeps,
    and its roster sent to the others, each of which drops the peer
    through one window when the signer may remove it (its admitter, a
    trust root, or the node itself). Tombstones are kept for good,
    1024 at most and 64 per signer, every roster carries them all, and
    no sync adds a removed key again.
  * The members' channel is split as an invite is: keel-mesh-members,
    the root helper (started by keel-mesh-members.path once the node is
    in a mesh), starts the listener keel-mesh-members-listen with a
    dynamic user, no capability and a sandbox, on TCP 51821 of the
    overlay address only, bound to the overlay's interface, so
    WireGuard authenticates who speaks, behind the invite listener's
    limits: a deadline per request, 4 connections and 2 per source,
    size caps, and sources refused too often. keel's firewall accepts
    that port on the overlay's interface only.
  * The mesh's identity no longer splits. Only a join or the operator's
    --adopt sets it: keel mesh invite on a node whose peers hold another
    identity, or with peers and none of its own, is refused; keel mesh
    create --adopt gives a mesh built by hand its identity, keel mesh
    sync --adopt ADDRESS takes a member's, which repairs a split. keel
    mesh status shows the identity.
  * keel mesh invite and join find this node's endpoint on a DHCP or
    SLAAC host: the uplink's global, stable IPv6 address (never a
    unique local, deprecated or privacy one, nor one of wg*), and a
    public IPv4 one, in the order of keel-core's console banner. They
    say which, and --endpoint still overrides. With only a privacy or
    an RFC 1918 address, invite is refused and asks for --endpoint.
  * keel mesh join, create and accept no longer print apply's "reverts
    in 120 s unless keel network confirm" while they confirm their own
    change: they say "confirming over the overlay…" and the result, and
    show apply's lines only when the change is not confirmed.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sat, 03 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000

keel (0.17.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The second part of handbook decision 0048, joining the mesh with one
    command. keel mesh create makes the mesh on its first node, a random
    unique local /64 with the node at ::1, and confirms it itself once
    the route check finds the gateways and the operator's SSH client
    still on the uplink (0048 amending 0018). keel mesh invite starts
    a root helper as the transient unit keel-mesh-invite@<id> and, from
    it, the unprivileged listener keel-mesh-listen@<id> (DynamicUser, no
    capability, sandboxed), HTTPS with the invite's own certificate on
    TCP 51820 by default (--port), its TLS key handed over as a memfd on
    a unix socket in its own runtime directory, the peer checked against
    the unit's MainPID; the HMAC key never leaves the root side, and a
    join's confirmation rests on a WireGuard handshake from the new
    peer's key. The port is opened in keel's
    firewall only where keel-firewall is enabled, through a new named
    set of the derived ruleset. The printed line feeds the token on
    standard input (keel mesh join - <<< keel1:...). keel mesh join
    joins: the request pinned to the token's fingerprint and signed with
    an HMAC of the invite's key, checked in constant time on the root
    side, once, without replays; each node writes the other
    into its own spec and applies it under the window, and the join's
    own session over the overlay confirms both windows. Tested end to
    end over 250 ms links with 2% loss. When the port cannot be reached, join
    applies its side and prints keel mesh accept keel1a:<answer> for the
    inviter. keel mesh status shows the peers, their handshakes and the
    pending invites, never a secret. docs/mesh.md.

