keel (0.13.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The instance spec gains appliance, installation and overlays (decision
    0041, step 3 of its first implementation). A spec that names an
    appliance is held against the manifests installed under the root:
    every overlay of the resolved chain is written out, enabled or
    disabled, and the requires of an enabled overlay are enabled (rule
    25); a secret a manifest declares is a known name, rendered as
    KEEL_SECRET_<NAME>, and generate is refused where the manifest says
    never (rule 26); app.options are the declared options, of their
    type, with every required one present (rule 27). on, off and ask are
    refused with the reason. spec validate takes --root.
  * apply --system converges each overlay's units: enabled enables and
    starts them, disabled stops and disables them, in requires order.
    Nothing is masked or unmasked; a masked unit is refused. CrowdSec's
    identity is made on its first enable (tracker#47): cscli machines
    add, an empty online_api_credentials.yaml, cscli capi register, whose
    failure offline is said and survived since the local API runs
    without it, and cscli bouncers add, whose key goes from its standard
    output to the bouncer's .local file, mode 0600, and nowhere else. No
    identity is made under --root. The bouncer always runs in nftables
    mode: its iptables mode, which the Debian postinst picks under
    TurnKey's iptables-legacy, needs ipset, which nothing installs.
  * apply --system writes Monit's checks derived from the manifests to
    /etc/keel/monit/keel-manifest.conf: the appliance's own processes and
    checks and those of every enabled overlay, restarted through systemd
    within each process's restart limit, and told through keel notify,
    whose new checks service and restarts name the unit and where to
    look. The file is always written and included from /etc/monit/conf.d
    only while monitor.enabled is true, then checked with monit -t and
    monit reloaded.
  * inspect writes appliance.name, installation.mode (from the spec the
    installer emitted) and the overlays' states from systemd. diff
    compares the states with systemd and the derived Monit file and its
    include with what apply renders, so a hand edit of either is drift.

