keel (0.11.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * apply makes the MariaDB server it converges answer before asking it
    anything: one that is starting is waited for, one that is stopped is
    started with systemctl start, both within one bound of 300 s. At
    first boot inithooks.service is not ordered after mariadb.service,
    so the hook 10keel-system asked the server while it was starting,
    the field was refused, the hook carried on as a first boot hook
    must, and the appliance kept server_id = 1 until apply ran again.
  * A server that does not answer within the bound, whose start fails,
    or that is active and refuses the client fails the field with that
    reason: nothing is written or restarted and the run exits non zero,
    so the next apply takes the field up again. A dry run starts no
    server, and under --root none is asked, started or waited for.
  * Before the wait, apply prints one line on the terminal (standard
    output when it is one, else /dev/tty, where the first boot dialogs
    draw), never into the output 10keel-system reads through a pipe, so
    tty1 no longer stays blank while the server starts.
  * A declared server whose unit is disabled is enabled, a line of the
    plan, so it starts at boot and not only when apply starts it.

