keel (0.11.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A MariaDB replica is read only (tracker#26). The replica's drop-in
    says read_only = ON, so the application on a replica can no longer
    write rows of its own: one WordPress page request inserted a
    wp_navigation post, the primary's next write reused its id, and the
    SQL thread stopped with error 1062. The replication thread and root
    write through it (READ_ONLY ADMIN, measured on MariaDB 11.8, where
    SUPER alone no longer does); the application's account holds
    privileges on its own schema only and is refused with error 1290.
    The restart that turns it on comes before the seed, whose load runs
    as root.
  * On a replica, after the seed, apply revokes READ_ONLY ADMIN with
    sql_log_bin off from every account that holds it but root, mysql
    and mariadb.sys at this machine: the image's admin and Adminer's
    adminer hold ALL ON *.*, and Adminer could write to the replica
    through them. The accounts are recorded in
    /var/lib/keel/database/read-only-admin first, and a promotion or a
    return to standalone or primary grants it back to those only. root,
    and Webmin's MySQL module with it, still writes: MariaDB 11.8 has no
    way to stop it, and docs/apply.md says so.
  * apply sets read_only with SET GLOBAL when the file already says so
    and the server does not (turned off by hand, or a standalone that
    was a replica), and says so when the server names no read_only.
  * keel database promote no longer loses what the replica received and
    had not applied: it stops the I/O thread, waits up to 600 s for the
    SQL thread to apply the relay log, and only then runs STOP SLAVE,
    RESET SLAVE ALL and turns read_only off. A SQL thread stopped on an
    error is refused with what to do, and one that stops or does not
    catch up while draining starts the I/O thread again, says whether
    that worked, and promotes nothing. The read_only line leaves the
    drop-in without a restart, before READ_ONLY ADMIN is granted back,
    so a failed GRANT cannot leave the new primary read only at its
    next restart.
  * keel inspect reports database.server.read_only, with the accounts
    that write through it, and keel diff compares it with the role the
    server has (decision 0020), not with a field of the description.
  * docs/apply.md: a replica is a warm standby and must not receive
    traffic until promoted, and what WordPress shows on one.

