keel (0.11.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Seeding a replica reads the primary's accounts before the dump, and
    again after it, and refuses when the two readings differ. Read only
    after the dump, a CREATE USER in between was applied twice and
    stopped the replica; read only before, it was never applied.
  * An account the replica holds with other grants than the primary's
    has its grants made the primary's, with sql_log_bin off, before
    replication starts: the primary's next REVOKE of a grant the replica
    lacked stopped it ("There is no such grant"). It keeps the replica's
    own authentication, since every node makes its own application
    password at first boot, and the plan names such accounts: a later
    ALTER USER of them on the primary replaces the replica's password,
    and the replica's application configuration must follow.
  * A primary that holds roles, or grants privileges to PUBLIC, is
    refused while apply observes the replica, before anything changes.
    Roles are not copied: a user whose privileges come through a role
    was copied with none, silently, and a change to a role stopped the
    replica.
  * The server id of a node with a WireGuard overlay is derived from its
    machine-id, its overlay address(es) and the listen entries that name
    it, leaving out wildcards and loopback: listen is :: on every node,
    so two nodes built from one image took the same server id. A node
    without an overlay keeps the server id it had. docs/apply.md says
    what changes a server id and what that does to a running pair.

