keel (0.11.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A new MariaDB replica is seeded with a copy of its primary before it
    replicates. It used to start from an empty gtid_slave_pos, so only
    what the primary wrote afterwards arrived: a WordPress replica had
    no wp_* tables, and the first UPDATE of an older post stopped its
    SQL thread. apply --system now pulls mariadb-dump
    --single-transaction --gtid --master-data=2 from the primary, as the
    replication account, drops the local databases the operator
    confirmed only once the copy is on disk, loads it and starts
    replication at the dump's GTID position (MASTER_USE_GTID=slave_pos).
    The password reaches the clients in an options file of mode 0600,
    created exclusively and removed afterwards, never an argument.
  * The primary is asked first, while apply observes the machine: an
    unreachable primary, or one that does not grant the copy, is refused
    before the replica's configuration is rewritten or anything dropped.
    A replica already replicating healthily from the declared primary is
    never dialled or copied again.
  * A primary grants the replication account SELECT, SHOW VIEW, TRIGGER
    and EVENT beside REPLICATION SLAVE, which is what the dump needs and
    no more than the binary log already carries to it. GRANT only adds,
    so a primary set up by an older keel gains them at its next apply.
  * The primary's accounts come with the copy: the ones the replica
    lacks, other than root, mysql, mariadb.sys, debian-sys-maint, repl,
    the anonymous account and roles, are read with SHOW CREATE USER and
    SHOW GRANTS and created with sql_log_bin off before replication
    starts, so an ALTER USER of the application's database user no
    longer stops the replica.
  * The seed is refused before anything is dropped when the data
    directory has less free space than twice the dump.
  * docs/apply.md states what the grant exposes: repl reads every table
    of the primary, password hashes included, and why that is the grant
    rather than one per schema.
  * A replica whose SQL thread stopped is refused with the server's
    error, and rebuilt from a fresh copy with --destroy-local-database:
    the way out for a replica 0.11.2 or older built empty.

