    container whose uplink is managed_by: host: wg-quick down on the old
    file, wg-quick up on the new one, both directions alike; a change
    that created the file reverts by removing it. The boot unit restores
    it before network-pre.target. wg-quick@<if> is enabled once the
    change is confirmed. One change waits in the window at a time, so an
    overlay change is refused in a run whose uplink moves.
  * The private key is made with wg genkey on the machine itself, mode
    0600, at the first converge (--skip-network included) or by keel
    network wireguard key, never under --root (keel-core#8). It is never
    printed and never in the file: PostUp gives the key file to wg set.
  * keel network confirm accepts, for an overlay change, a new session
    arriving at an overlay address the change declares, or at an address
    another interface holds (the uplink), and says which path it tested.
    It dates the change on the process start clock, which is right in a
    container, where lxcfs's /proc/uptime is not.
  * In a container whose host has not loaded the wireguard module, apply
    refuses and names modprobe wireguard on the host; inspect reports it.
  * keel inspect reads the overlay back from /etc/wireguard with the
    public key wg pubkey gives, never the private key; keel diff compares
    peers by public key.
  * New commands: keel network wireguard key (this node's public key,
    making the pair first) and keel network wireguard suggest-address (a
    random unique local /64 for the first node of a set).
  * Suggests: wireguard-tools.
  * From the review of #49: no route of the overlay may capture the
    uplink. The overlay addresses and every allowed_ips prefix must be
    private (fc00::/7; RFC 1918 or 100.64.0.0/10), which refuses a /0
    and a public /1 pair, and none may overlap an address prefix or
    gateway of network.interfaces or a peer's endpoint address. keel
    network confirm refuses an overlay change, from a console too, while
    ip route get sends a declared or live default gateway, or an uplink
    session's client, through the overlay, or gives no answer for one.
    A zoned gateway (fe80::1%eth0) is asked without its zone, and a
    default route via inet6 fe80::1 (RFC 5549) reads as fe80::1.
  * A wg0.conf with an inline PrivateKey keeps its identity: apply moves
    the key into the key file (0600, written to a temporary file and
    linked into place, never printed) before rewriting the file, instead
    of making a new one, and refuses when the key file holds another
    key.
  * A revert whose marker cannot be read restores the saved copy to the
    file recorded beside it (saved.json), and restores nothing when that
    is unknown, never an overlay's file over /etc/network/interfaces.
  * apply reads the overlay interface as well as its file: a confirmed
    overlay that is down is drift and is restarted; a right file never
    brought up here comes up under the window, and its revert leaves it
    down.
  * New apply flag --skip-uplink: converge the overlay and leave
    network.interfaces alone, for confconsole's overlay screen.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 05:47:51 +0000

keel (0.10.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Declared nameservers are no longer dropped for their family. ifupdown
    writes them only in a static stanza, two per stanza, and resolvconf
    takes them from any stanza, so when one family is static and the
    other is not, the static stanza carries the servers of both families,
    two of them, the first of each family before a second of either. A
    VM with IPv4 static and IPv6 auto lost 2606:4700:4700::1111 this way.
    This changes the conf keel spec apply renders; the first boot conf is
    inithooks' 00declarative's. The servers the file cannot hold are
    named by keel spec apply as a warning and by apply --system in its
    plan (keel#45).
  * Breaks: inithooks (<< 2.3.6+keel9~). An older 01ipconfig is fatal on
    an IPv4 nameserver in IP6_DNS* and ignores IP6_SLAAC; keel still does
    not depend on inithooks.
  * A network change and its revert give the interface's IPv6 autoconf
    setting back before ifup on a file that keeps SLAAC: ifupdown-ng runs
    no post-down for an interface whose up failed, so a slaac: false file
    that failed to come up would leave SLAAC off until a reboot. The
    setting before the change is recorded in the pending marker.
  * The sysctl key is written with slashes, so a VLAN name such as eth0.45
    is one component; inspect reads either form.
  * apply --system keeps a file's "inet6 auto" for a spec's method: auto
    instead of rewriting it as "inet6 dhcp".
  * New field network.interfaces.<if>.ipv6.slaac (default true, only with
    method: static). slaac: false renders IP6_SLAAC=no, which inithooks
    2.3.6+keel9 writes as pre-up and post-down sysctl lines turning
    autoconf off, so the static address is the only one; inspect reads it
    back, and diff compares an absent slaac as true. With an older
    inithooks, apply refuses rather than write a file that keeps SLAAC.
  * keel network confirm: a session of a family with a declared static
    address must arrive at that address, not merely at one the interface
    holds, since with SLAAC kept a confirm could arrive over the SLAAC
    address while the static one is broken. A static address of the other
    family is reported as not tested.

