keel (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel spec apply --system-only configures a MariaDB server in the role
    database.server declares: the server id, the addresses it answers on
    and the binary log in one drop-in, restarting the server only when
    that file changed; a grant of replication per entry of allowed_from
    on a primary, in the host pattern spelling MariaDB has for a prefix,
    with an origin the description dropped withdrawn; and CHANGE MASTER
    TO with MASTER_USE_GTID on a replica. Phase 3 of handbook decision
    0013, MariaDB first.
  * Becoming a replica destroys the local database, so apply refuses
    unless the server holds no database but its own, or the operator
    passes --destroy-local-database in that invocation. A server that
    cannot be asked what it holds is refused too, pointing a replica at a
    different primary is the same act, and nothing else in keel passes
    the flag, so a first boot cannot destroy anything.
  * apply never promotes a replica and never demotes a primary. Where the
    observed role would need either, it refuses and names the operation.
    This is the acting side of the rule docs/diff.md already states.
  * The server id comes from /etc/machine-id and the declared listen
    addresses, and never from the description alone: two appliances
    deployed from one description would collide, and two nodes with the
    same server id stop replicating. Both sources, because the published
    core layer ships a populated machine-id, so every appliance assembled
    from it holds the same value; measured on two nodes of the gate, which
    took the same server id from it. The addresses a pair answers on
    differ by construction on one /64.
  * The replication credential is the only secret --system-only resolves,
    and it is the only one it needs. It never reaches an argument vector
    or the printed plan, it is escaped as a MariaDB literal, and a file
    holding a control character is refused rather than mangled.
  * The line that confirms the destruction repeats the reason and not the
    remedy: a run that went ahead telling the operator that nothing was
    changed would be a lie. Measured on the build host, where it said both.
  * New actions: RunSql, statements on standard input whose description
    never shows them, and Refuse, a step that did not happen and says why,
    counted as a failure so the exit code tells the truth.

