keel (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel diff compares the database section. The engine, the role and the
    listen addresses are compared against what the server says it is; the
    endpoint a replica replicates from and the origins a primary authorizes
    are compared when the declared role has a use for them; and the
    credentials, being references whose values are never read, are not
    compared at all.
  * A declared replica against an observed primary is drift that carries a
    warning saying never to correct it automatically, because demoting a
    primary destroys everything written to it since the replica last agreed.
    The mirror image, a declared primary against an observed replica, carries
    its own: promoting splits the pair into two writable servers. docs/diff.md
    states this as a rule of the project, for the phase that will configure
    replication and not only read it. The warning is in the JSON document as
    well, so a caller shows it without reproducing it.
  * A field the declared role has no use for is not compared: a standalone may
    carry the authorizations it will need as a primary, the way a certificate
    configuration waits behind tls.acme.enabled. The role itself is always
    compared. Where the declared and observed roles disagree, the role line
    carries the drift and the field it governs is unknown, naming the role the
    machine is in, so one fact produces one drift line.
  * Listen addresses and authorized origins compare as sets and in canonical
    form. A name is never resolved: an authorization that names a host and a
    server that holds an address are two different things, and resolving
    before comparing is how a name that stopped resolving would pass as same.
  * inspect says why a field of another role was not read, so the reason
    reaches the diff line.
  * MariaDB is asked for its replica status without --skip-column-names.
    SHOW REPLICA STATUS\G prints one "Name: value" per line and that flag
    takes the names away, so the primary's address arrived as a bare line and
    the reading found no primary on a machine that was plainly replicating.
    Found on the bench, which is why the bench is part of this issue: the
    fixtures agreed with the code. The role no longer depends on a value
    happening to contain a colon either; any output at all is a replica row,
    because SHOW REPLICA STATUS prints nothing on a server that replicates
    from nowhere.
  * allowed_from accepts a host pattern, 2001:db8:1:%, which is the only
    spelling MariaDB has for an IPv6 prefix in a grant: it takes an address and
    a netmask for IPv4 alone. Found on the bench, on a primary authorized from
    a /64, where inspect read the pattern off the server and the description it
    wrote did not validate. A test now asserts that every origin the reading
    can produce is one the schema accepts, on both SQL engines.

